---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
---
<Layout
  title="Hanfu Colors: What Each Color Means"
  description="The Hanfu color system: red for joy, yellow for emperors, blue for spring, white for mourning. Understand color meanings, dynasty palettes, and how to choose colors with confidence."
  canonicalUrl="https://www.dresses-hanfu.com/guides/colors/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Guides", url: "/guides" }, { name: "Hanfu Colors", url: "/guides/colors" }]}
  articleSchema={{
    datePublished: "2026-08-01",
    dateModified: "2026-08-08",
    author: { "@type": "Person", "name": "Hanfu Dress Guide Editorial Team" }
  }}
>
  <SiteHeader active="/guides/how-to-wear" />
  <main>
    <article>
      <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;"><a href="/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Hanfu Colors</p>
          <h1>Hanfu Colors: The Language of Chinese Color</h1>
          <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
            In traditional Chinese culture, color carried meaning the way text does today. Red meant joy, yellow meant imperial power, white meant mourning. Choosing Hanfu colors is therefore both an aesthetic and a cultural decision — and this guide gives you the full palette.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>The Core Meanings</h2>
          <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
            <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
              <thead><tr><th>Color</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Best For</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Red (hong)</strong></td><td>Joy, celebration, prosperity, good fortune, vitality</td><td>Weddings, New Year, festivals</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Yellow (huang)</strong></td><td>The imperial color — divine authority, reserved for the emperor</td><td>Formal photoshoots; avoid for ordinary occasions</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Purple (zi)</strong></td><td>Nobility, dignity, spiritual wisdom</td><td>Formal ceremonies</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Green (lv)</strong></td><td>Spring, renewal, harmony with nature</td><td>Garden and outdoor events</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Blue (lan)</strong></td><td>Heaven, tranquility, scholarly refinement</td><td>Cultural events, tea ceremonies</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>White (bai)</strong></td><td>Purity, but also mourning in traditional context</td><td>Under-layers and casual wear; not for festive formal events</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Black (xuan)</strong></td><td>Wisdom, water, the north</td><td>Formal Han-dynasty ritual style</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Pink (fen)</strong></td><td>Softness, youth, romance</td><td>Modern and casual Hanfu</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">The Five Elements Color System</h2>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Wuxing: elements and their colors</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Traditional Chinese cosmology linked the five elements (wuxing) to colors, directions, and dynasties. Wood was green/blue (east, spring), fire was red (south, summer), earth was yellow (center), metal was white (west, autumn), and water was black (north, winter). Each dynasty chose an "official" color based on which element it claimed, which is why dynastic palettes differ.
          </p>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">Palettes by Dynasty</h2>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Signature colors by era</h3>
          <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
            <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
              <thead><tr><th>Dynasty</th><th>Signature Colors</th><th>Feeling</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Han</strong></td><td>Black, dark red, earthen tones</td><td>Solemn, ritual, restrained</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Tang</strong></td><td>Red, gold, emerald, purple, sapphire</td><td>Bold, opulent, cosmopolitan</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Song</strong></td><td>Ivory, pale blue, grey, soft brown</td><td>Refined, muted, scholarly</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Ming</strong></td><td>Deep blues, bright red, gold, rich embroidery</td><td>Structured, dignified, vibrant</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Modern</strong></td><td>Anything — plus navy, dusty pink, pastels</td><td>Personal, casual, expressive</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">How to Choose Hanfu Colors</h2>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Five ways to choose well</h3>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li><strong>Match the occasion.</strong> Red for celebrations and weddings, subdued tones for ceremonies and tea events, bright for festivals.</li>
            <li><strong>Think about your skin tone.</strong> Warm undertones pop with reds, golds, and warm greens; cool undertones glow with blues, purples, and pinks.</li>
            <li><strong>Follow the dynasty.</strong> If you're wearing a Song-style outfit, the muted Song palette will look most coherent; a Tang silhouette in pale ivory looks off-brand.</li>
            <li><strong>Layer with contrast.</strong> The classic combo pairs a darker outer robe with a lighter inner layer, or a bright accent color with neutral base.</li>
            <li><strong>Use color to balance.</strong> Wide sleeves and long skirts are already statement-making; one accent color (scarf, sash, shoes) is often enough.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">One Rule to Remember</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Yellow is historically the emperor's color. Modern wearers do wear yellow Hanfu for photoshoots — no law against it — but in formal, culturally respectful contexts, bright yellow is best left out. And white: beautiful for under-layers and modern casual wear, but avoid all-white for weddings and festive celebrations, where it reads as mourning.
          </p>

          <div style="margin-top:3rem;padding:2rem;background:var(--color-accent-light);border-radius:var(--radius-md);">
            <p style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;font-family:var(--font-display);">Keep Reading</p>
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.75rem;margin-top:1rem;">
              <a href="/guides/meaning/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Meaning</a>
              <a href="/guides/fabrics/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Fabrics</a>
              <a href="/guides/how-to-wear/" class="btn btn-outline">How to Wear</a>
              <a href="/styles/tang/" class="btn btn-outline">Tang Style Hanfu</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>